<b>יג.</b> Parshat ha-Akida - first the parshat of the sacrifices. and can recite Parshat HaAkida and Parshat Haman even on Shabbat. And the saying is not enough, but let him observe what he says and know the wonders of Hashem. And also what they said in the Gemara: "Anyone who says glory to David three times every day is guaranteed to be a son of the next world", also in this way. And there is a reason for saying all this, because Parshat Akida, to remember the right of the ancestors every day, and also to subdue his desire, as Yitzchak gave his soul. And the Parshatha Haman, so that he believes that all his sustenance is in vain under private supervision, and as the saying goes: "He who has much has not favored and he who has little has not lacked", to teach that the abundance of intercession is of no benefit to a nation. And ita in Yerushalmi blessings: everyone who recites Parshat Haman, he is guaranteed that his sustenance will not decrease. And the Ten mitzvot, so that he remembers every day the status of Mount Sinai and his faith in Hashem is strengthened. And the parsha of the sacrifices, Damrinan in the offerings: "This is the law of sinning", everyone who deals with the law of sin is as if the sacrificer is sinning, etc.<br>
<br>
<b>יד.</b> And complete - and the same is true of Parshat Toda, a model is at all complete. And after he ascends and makes peace and gives thanks, he will say Parashat Neskim, because there is no sacrifice without Neskim. But you have sinned and are guilty, no one should agree.<br>
<br>
<b>טו.</b> And you sinned - refer to Magen Avraham who wrote, if he knows that we will commit a sin, he will say the parashat sinned first. And what the rest of the judges say does not mean yes.<br>
<br>
<b>טז.</b> In the crowd - from the unbelievers, who will say: There is no Torah but this one. And especially in our time. And that's why they don't write it as well on the special counter for the public. Some have written that it is not forbidden to say it in public, but to state it between the blessings like a Shema reading; But first Baruch said, it is allowed even in public. Vanan Nectinen forbids gathering altogether. And in the singular, precisely not within the prayer; But determining within the blessings, even for an individual is prohibited [AT and ARAH].
